[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Hierarchical Data Structures: Trees and Tries"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Hierarchical Problem Solving"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Hierarchical data structures are foundational tools in computer science, enabling efficient organization, retrieval, and manipulation of data that exhibits parent-child relationships. Unlike linear data structures such as arrays or linked lists, trees and tries allow algorithms to navigate complex search spaces logarithmically or proportionally to key lengths."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts: Trees"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"A tree is a collection of nodes connected by directed edges. The topmost node is called the root, and nodes with no children are called leaves. Binary Search Trees (BSTs) enforce an ordering property where the left child's value is less than the parent, and the right child's value is greater, optimizing search operations."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"class TreeNode:
    def __init__(self, val=0):
        self.val = val
        self.left = None
        self.right = None"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts: Tries"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"A trie (prefix tree) is a specialized tree used to store associative data structures where the keys are usually strings. Tries optimize string lookups and prefix matching, providing O(M) time complexity, where M is the length of the string, independent of the number of strings stored."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"class TrieNode:
    def __init__(self):
        self.children = {}
        self.is_end_of_word = False"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Problem Solving Strategies"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Depth-First Search (DFS) for path-sum or tree-traversal problems where you need to explore every branch completely."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Breadth-First Search (BFS) for level-order traversal and finding the shortest path in unweighted tree structures."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Apply Tries for autocomplete systems, spell checkers, and longest prefix matching algorithms."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering trees and tries significantly enhances your problem-solving toolkit. By recognizing the underlying hierarchical nature of a problem, you can apply these structures to reduce time complexity and write scalable, elegant code."}]
